WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.920
You can animate with sprites too.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.640
To do this, you'll need to update their properties

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.040
inside the draw loop using the counter pattern.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.840
For example, this program creates a sprite called mySprite.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.280
Then inside the draw loop, it uses the counter pattern

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.280
to increase the sprite's x position by one in each frame.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.840
This makes the sprite move smoothly across the screen.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:38.760
Adding a second sprite to a program is easy.

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.580
You just use the createSprite command again.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.040
This program animates two sprites

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.240
called sprite1 and sprite2.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.160
sprite1 uses the counter pattern to move across the screen

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:53.720
by adding one to its x property.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:57.160
sprite2 moves down by adding one to the y property

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:59.080
on each tick of the draw loop.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:02.600
These sprites have lots of other properties,

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.280
but our program only needs to deal

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:06.160
with the ones that are changing.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.840
Sprites already help you write more readable programs,

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.000
and they are going to be extremely helpful

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.760
and powerful tools for making fun games and animations.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:18.760
So stay tuned.
